$DIR/main.p:7:15: cannot store f(C{}) in package variable saved, which outlives main's frame
    f(C{}) returns a closure, which it builds, with the variables it uses, in a
    region of its caller's frame, main's (at main.p:7); they live as long as
    that frame (section 11.5). When main returns, its frame is reclaimed, but
    package variables live for the whole program, so package variable saved
    would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of main's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as package variable saved.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
